    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "88d4095b_b044836b",
        "filename": "power/intel_x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1260470
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-05T21:02:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah... to me it seems like a tradeoff. I\u0027ll post my defense of doing it this way and then we can decide:\n1. common_intel_x86_power_handle_state() is already getting a little hairy, and I didn\u0027t want to sprinkle a bunch more ifdefs into it. Keeping all the code here made it very easy to reason about, and easy to excise with a feature macro.\n2. It seemed cleaner to me to have this code run in response to power signal changes, rather than the while(1) grinding that power_handle_state() seems to do. I guess it\u0027s not a whole lot of code, but it still seemed nice to keep that load off the EC.\n3. The other nice part about having a callout when the power signals change is that you don\u0027t have to go fishing through chipset_get_sleep_signal(), you can just operate on the old and new arguments containing all the power signal states.",
      "parentUuid": "0f2a65df_fdccd677",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "01af02670e8db2673242e01b7dd0c613a11860f4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0974044c_e45c13b7",
        "filename": "power/intel_x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-05T22:07:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understand the intent to keep things separate, but in practice this handling is not separate from the power state machine itself. We already have a notion of power state and adding sub states which are completely separate from that adds more complexity than is required.\n\n1. You can always add empty functions with #ifs outside the common_intel_x86_power_handle_state if that function seems ugly.\n2. Even though it is a while(1) loop, that task just calls task_wait_event(-1) in power_common_state to wait for an actual event change to take action. So, it won\u0027t change the EC load.\n3. You can always use power_get_state if you don\u0027t want to query the sleep signal. In fact if the calls are made from appropriate states in common_intel_x86_power_handle_state, there is no need to check for sleep signals since the power state guarantees state of the signal.",
      "parentUuid": "88d4095b_b044836b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "01af02670e8db2673242e01b7dd0c613a11860f4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
